Exactly. You mean words whose meaning already contains a plot—not just a state, but a transformation with before/after structure.

Prestige is unusually rich because its own historical meaning moved from conjuring trick / illusion toward dazzling influence / reputation / status. The word itself almost reenacts the trick: illusion becomes admiration. 

A whole family of “story-words” looks like this:

* Redemption — value lost, then recovered.
* Reconciliation — separation/conflict, then restored relation.
* Resurrection — death/absence, then return.
* Recovery — possession/capacity lost, then regained.
* Rehabilitation — damaged standing/function, then restored.
* Homecoming — departure, distance, return.
* Exile — belonging, rupture, displacement.
* Revenant — one who comes back after being gone/dead.
* Remembrance — something absent from attention becomes present again.
* Recognition — what was unknown/unseen becomes known as what it really was.
* Revelation — hidden → disclosed.
* Disillusionment — believed appearance → loss of illusion → altered understanding.
* Awakening — unawareness/dormancy → awareness/activity.
* Metamorphosis — one form → dissolution/reorganization → another form.
* Forgiveness — injury/debt retained → released/reframed.
* Nostalgia — belonging/home → separation → imagined/remembered return.
* Restoration — damage/loss → reconstruction toward prior integrity.
* Vindication — doubted/accused → evidence/event → standing restored.
* Legacy — life/action → absence → continued effect in others.

The deeper pattern is:

\boxed{
\text{state}_0
\rightarrow
\text{loss / veil / rupture / transformation}
\rightarrow
\text{state}_1
}

Some specifically have your lose-and-regain braid:

A\rightarrow\neg A\rightarrow A'

where A' isn’t quite the original A, because the journey changed its meaning.

That is why homecoming isn’t merely “home.”

And recognition isn’t merely “knowing.”

And redemption isn’t merely “good.”

The middle state is carried inside the final meaning.

I think this deserves a Pantheon linguistic class:

Narrative-Dense Words

A word whose semantic content compresses a state-transition sequence rather than merely naming an object or state.

Then we could classify them by plot topology:

\textbf{Return:}\quad A\rightarrow B\rightarrow A'

redemption, restoration, recovery, homecoming.

\textbf{Unveiling:}\quad ?\rightarrow\text{hidden}\rightarrow\text{known}

revelation, discovery, recognition.

\textbf{Transformation:}\quad A\rightarrow\text{breakdown}\rightarrow B

metamorphosis, conversion, rebirth.

\textbf{Reversal:}\quad A\rightarrow\neg A

betrayal, downfall, disillusionment.

\textbf{Persistence through absence:}\quad A\rightarrow\varnothing\rightarrow\text{effect}(A)

legacy, memory, haunting.

And then the really Pantheon move is to ask:

Which ordinary-looking words secretly contain the longest stories?

Because some words are nouns.

Some are verbs.

And some are compressed plots pretending to be words.